{
  "summary": "The previous rounds have converged on the right decomposition axes but keep debating extraction order without resolving the fundamental tension that makes this refactor genuinely hard: PipelineApp is not just a God Class — it is a God Class whose methods are non-extractable by design due to Textual's @work decorator constraint. Round 4 correctly identifies that @work(thread=True) methods must live on an App or Widget subclass, but then dismisses the problem by suggesting call_from_thread is sufficient glue. It is not. The real constraint is threefold: (1) @work-decorated methods participate in Textual's worker lifecycle (cancellation via worker.cancel(), group exclusivity via exclusive=True, the workers property for introspection), (2) the 9 @work methods use self.call_from_thread extensively (57 call sites total) to mutate reactive attributes and trigger UI updates atomically within the UI thread, and (3) several workers check self._plan_running / self._implement_running flags that are themselves reactive attributes driving CSS class changes on widgets. Any extraction that moves these methods off PipelineApp must either (a) create a Widget subclass that gets mounted invisibly as a 'headless controller widget' — ugly but preserves full Textual worker semantics including cancellation and group exclusivity — or (b) abandon @work entirely and manage threading manually with asyncio.to_thread plus explicit app.call_from_thread callbacks, which loses worker cancellation, group exclusivity, and the workers introspection API that the kill_agent action depends on (line 3247: it iterates self.workers to cancel running agents). Option (a) is the pragmatic path: create a PipelineWorkerWidget(Widget) that hosts all @work methods and gets mounted as a hidden child of PipelineApp. This preserves every Textual semantic, keeps the worker lifecycle intact, and the extraction is mechanical — move the methods, change self references to self.app where they touch PipelineApp state, done. This is the Tier 2 extraction that should happen before modal extraction (contra every prior proposal), because the 9 @work methods plus their 57 call_from_thread sites are 60% of the actual complexity in PipelineApp, whereas modals are already self-contained classes that just happen to be in the same file.\n\nOn the _check_for_changes scalability issue: Round 2 correctly notes that <100 features per board makes per-file stat viable, and Round 3 identifies the real bug (FeatureFile.list_all() re-scanning all boards on every change cycle for server push). But none of the rounds address the simpler optimization that eliminates most stat calls entirely: check the board directory's mtime first (a single stat call), and only proceed to per-file stats if the directory mtime changed. Directory mtime updates on any file create/delete/rename within it on both Linux and macOS, which covers the common case of phase transitions (which rename/move JSON files between directories). The only case this misses is in-place content edits that don't change the directory mtime — but those are rare in this system since phase transitions are the primary mutation. This single-stat short-circuit plus caching the list_all() result (refreshed only on board switch or explicit refresh) would reduce the polling cost from O(n) stats per tick to O(1) in the common no-change case, without introducing filesystem watches or any new dependencies.\n\nOn the STAGE_ACTIONS duplication that Round 4 identifies: the two STAGE_ACTIONS (one in state.py for pipeline phase validation, one in PipelineApp for keybinding dispatch) serve different purposes and should not be unified into a single dispatch table. The state.py version defines which pipeline operations are valid for which stages (a domain rule), while the PipelineApp version maps keyboard shortcuts to UI actions for the current stage (a presentation concern). Merging them would couple domain validation to UI keybinding layout, making it impossible to change one without affecting the other. The right fix is to rename PipelineApp's version to STAGE_KEYBINDINGS to make the distinction explicit, and have each action_* method delegate stage validation to the state.py STAGE_ACTIONS rather than duplicating the check.\n\nRecommended concrete sequencing: (1) Rename STAGE_ACTIONS to STAGE_KEYBINDINGS in PipelineApp and unify stage validation to use state.py's STAGE_ACTIONS — this is a 30-minute mechanical change that removes ~40 lines of redundant guard clauses. (2) Add the directory-mtime short-circuit to _check_for_changes and cache list_all() — another small targeted fix. (3) Extract all @work methods into a PipelineWorkerWidget using the headless widget pattern — this is the high-value structural change. (4) Extract modals into tui_modals.py — safe, mechanical, high line-count reduction. (5) Extract pure helper functions into tui_helpers.py. Steps 1-2 are independent bug fixes that improve the code today regardless of whether the larger refactor proceeds. Steps 3-5 are the structural extraction, ordered by value delivered per unit of risk."
}
